.\" Hey, Emacs!  This is an -*- nroff -*- source file.
.TH "Window Maker" 1 "February 2015"
.SH NAME
wmaker \- X11 window manager with a NEXTSTEP look
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wmaker
.I "[-options]"
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
Window Maker is a X11 window manager with a NEXTSTEP look. It tries to
emulate NeXT's look as much as possible, but it deviates from it as
necessary.
.PP
Several keybindings can be configured to run external commands. The
.B LockScreenKey
/
.B LockScreenCommand
pair invokes a screen locker, and the
.B ClipboardKey
/
.B ClipboardCommand
pair invokes a clipboard manager. The
.B LockScreenCommand
defaults to
.BR wmlock ;
the clipboard pair defaults to
.I None
(empty/unbound). Set them in the
WindowMaker defaults domain. For the bundled
.BR wmclipd (1)
clipboard history manager, use:
.EX
ClipboardKey = "Mod4+v";
ClipboardCommand = "wmakermsg send_tick wmclipd-popup";
.EE
.PP
.B ClipboardFilenameTemplate
in the WindowMaker defaults domain controls the filename stem for
.BR wmclipd (1)
exports. It defaults to
.IR "clipboard_%Y-%m-%d_at_%H:%M:%S" ,
uses
.BR strftime (3)
with local time, and is reloaded for each save. The user value takes precedence
over the global value. Empty, invalid, overlong, or unsafe expansions are
rejected. The extension and collision suffix are added by wmclipd.
Exported files may contain sensitive clipboard contents.
.SH "ACCENT PICKER"
When Window Maker is configured with
.BR \-\-enable\-xkb\-multilingual ,
an optional Accent Picker can insert accented and other special
characters without changing the XKB layout. It is disabled by default so
upgrades never take a key away from applications. To enable the held
trigger, set in the WindowMaker defaults domain:
.EX
AccentPickerKey = "Menu";
.EE
Hold
.BR Menu ,
press a base key, tap the same base key again to cycle the variants,
and release
.B Menu
to insert the selected variant. Space, the arrow keys, and
.B j
/
.B k
also cycle. Escape cancels without inserting anything.
.PP
For modifier mode, map an unused key to Hyper outside Window Maker:
.EX
setxkbmap \-option caps:hyper
.EE
then set:
.EX
AccentPickerModifier = Hyper;
.EE
Hold Hyper and press a base key. Space cycles the variants and releasing
Hyper inserts the selection. Without a Space press the original base
character is inserted, so the feature never swallows an ordinary key.
.PP
The active character sets are chosen with
.BR AccentPickerSets ,
a proplist array of set names. The
.I auto
token derives sets from the configured XKB layouts, the
.I all
token enables every built-in set, and IPA is only enabled explicitly or
by
.IR all .
The default is:
.EX
AccentPickerSets = ("auto", "Currency");
.EE
Per-base overrides can be added in the
.B AccentPickerVariants
dictionary in the WindowMaker defaults domain.
.SH "SWITCH PANEL"
The switch panel (the Alt-Tab popup) can be placed on a chosen screen
position of a chosen head. Both settings live in the WindowMaker
defaults domain and are written with
.BR wdwrite (1).
Neither has a WPrefs panel, and changing them does not move a switch
panel that is already open. The new values apply on the next switch
panel invocation.
Removing either key on runtime reload retains its current value.
Writing an empty
.B SwitchPanelMonitor
explicitly restores pointer-head selection.
.PP
.B SwitchPanelPlacement
selects the position within the target head. Accepted values are
.BR center ,
.BR top ,
.BR bottom ,
.BR left ,
.BR right ,
.BR topleft ,
.BR topright ,
.BR bottomleft
and
.BR bottomright ,
matched case-insensitively.
.BR top ,
.BR bottom ,
.BR left
and
.BR right
mean the matching edge center. The default is
.BR center .
A panel that is too large for the head is anchored at the head origin
on the axes where it does not fit, keeping one icon visible whenever
the window list is not empty.
.PP
.B SwitchPanelMonitor
selects the head the panel appears on. The default is empty, which
targets the head holding the pointer. A value is one of the reserved
keywords, an escaped monitor name, a plain monitor name, or an index
token:
.IP \(bu 2
.BR Cursor ,
matched case-insensitively, is the reserved keyword for the head
holding the pointer, the same as the empty default.
.B Window
is the reserved keyword for the head holding the window that is
focused when the cycle starts. When there is no eligible focused
window, the pointer head is used instead. The keywords are recognized
before any name lookup, so they select their dynamic mode even when a
monitor carries the same name.
.IP \(bu 2
The case-sensitive prefix
.B name:
selects the exact RandR monitor name that follows it. Use it to target
a monitor whose name would otherwise read as a keyword or an index
token, for example
.B "name:Cursor"
or
.BR "name:#0" .
The name after the prefix is never interpreted, so
.B "name:name:foo"
selects a monitor actually called
.BR name:foo ,
while
.B name:foo
selects one called
.BR foo .
An empty
.B name:
is unresolvable.
.IP \(bu 2
Plain monitor names match exactly and case-sensitively, including
names that begin with
.BR # .
Names identify the current monitor objects, not a permanent physical
display identity. List them with
.BR "xrandr \-\-listactivemonitors" .
.IP \(bu 2
An index token is
.BI # N
with non-negative decimal digits only, and addresses the zero-based
head index
.I N
only when that head has no monitor name. So
.B #0
names an unnamed head, never a head that carries a name such as
.BR HDMI-1 .
Strings like
.BR #5abc ,
.BR #+5
and
.BR #-1
are not index tokens and resolve to nothing.
.PP
An unknown name, an empty escaped name, a malformed or out-of-range
index, or a name that disappears after a reconfiguration behaves as if
the setting were unset: the panel appears on the pointer head and a
diagnostic is logged once per unresolved value. The value itself is
kept in the domain, so it can resolve again after a topology change.
Head indices can change when monitors are added or removed. Prefer
monitor names. On a single-head setup without a monitor name, the
empty default and
.B #0
both refer to the whole screen.
.PP
The default configuration keeps the historical icon count heuristic.
Choosing a non-center placement, a named or indexed head, or a resolved
.B Window
head enables a fit that accounts for the full panel borders in device
pixels.
.B SwitchPanelIconSize
is accepted from 1 to 16667. Any other value logs a warning and uses
the default 64. The accepted range is a syntax and dimension bound,
not a promise that every size can show a panel: each panel raster or
pixmap is limited to 16 MiB and the estimated peak of the panel pixel
allocations to 64 MiB, and wrlib and the X protocol cap image and
window dimensions. A very large icon size or window count that exceeds
one of these limits logs the actual reason and falls back to cycling
without a visible panel. Icon sizes 1, 2 and 3 render real icons in
positive, bounded dimensions.
.SH "OPTIONS"
.TP
.BI \-\-clientid " id"
specify the XSMP session management client ID. This is normally passed
by the session manager when restarting Window Maker to restore the
saved session
.TP
.BI \-display " host:display.screen"
specify the display to use.
On multiheaded machines, Window Maker will automatically manage all screens.
If you want Window Maker to manage only a specific screen, you must supply
the screen number you want to have managed with the
.B \-display
command line argument. For example, if you want Window Maker to manage only
screen 1, start it as:
.EX
wmaker -display :0.1
.EE
.TP
.B \-\-dont\-restore
do not restore the saved session
.TP
.B \-\-get\-socketpath
print the path of the Unix-domain control socket for the screen named by
.B DISPLAY
and exit, without opening an X connection. Programs launched from the dock
or the root menu inherit this path in the
.B WMAKER_IPC_SOCKET
environment variable, and
.BR wmakermsg (1)
resolves it the same way.
.TP
.B \-\-global_defaults_path
print the path where the files for the default configuration are installed and exit
.TP
.B \-\-help
print the list of supported command line options, one per line, and exit
.TP
.BI \-\-locale " locale"
specify the locale (i18n language) to use; Window Maker will also set the variable
.B LANG
which will be inherited by all applications started from Window Maker
.TP
.B \-\-no\-autolaunch
do not launch at start-up the applications that were marked in the configuration as autolaunched
.TP
.B \-\-no\-clip
do not show the workspace Clip
.TP
.B \-\-no\-dock
do not show the application Dock
.TP
.B \-\-no\-drawer
disable the Drawers in the Dock
@!HAVE_INOTIFY@.TP
@!HAVE_INOTIFY@.B \-\-no\-polling
@!HAVE_INOTIFY@disable the periodic check on the configuration file to reload it automatically
@USE_ICCCM_WMREPLACE@.TP
@USE_ICCCM_WMREPLACE@.B \-\-replace
@USE_ICCCM_WMREPLACE@ask the currently running window manager to let Window Maker take its place
.TP
.B \-\-static
do not update or save automatically the configuration
.TP
.B \-\-version
display Window Maker's version number and exit
.TP
.B \-\-visual\-id
specify the ID of the visual to use; see
.BR xdpyinfo (1)
for a list of visuals available in your display
.PP
.SH FILES
The per-user configuration root is
.B ~/.config/WindowMaker
(or
.BR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/WindowMaker " when that variable is set)."
For backward compatibility,
.B ~/GNUstep
is used instead when a
.B ~/GNUstep/Defaults
directory already exists. The
.B WMAKER_USER_ROOT
environment variable overrides both.
.TP
.B ~/.config/WindowMaker/Defaults/WindowMaker
general Window Maker defaults.
.TP
.B ~/.config/WindowMaker/Defaults/WMState
information about the Dock and Clip. DON'T edit this while running
Window Maker. It will be overwritten.
.TP
.B ~/.config/WindowMaker/Defaults/WMRootMenu
Contains the name of the file to read the root menu from or the
menu itself, in property list format.
.TP
.B ~/.config/WindowMaker/Defaults/WMWindowAttributes
Attributes for different application classes and instances. Use the
Attribute Editor (right drag the application's title bar, select
Attributes) instead of modifying this file directly. There are just a
few options not available using the Attributes Editor.
.TP
.B @pkgconfdir@/
All the above-mentioned files are READ from here if not found except
for WMState, which is COPIED from here. No matter where they are read
from, if it's necessary to write configuration changes back into these
files, user's files will be written to.
.TP
.B ~/.config/WindowMaker/Library/WindowMaker/autostart
This script is automatically executed when Window Maker is started.
.TP
.B ~/.config/WindowMaker/Library/WindowMaker/exitscript
This script is automatically executed immediately before Window Maker is
exited.
.B Note:
If you need to run something that requires the X server to be running
from this script, make sure you do not use the
.I SHUTDOWN
command from the root menu to exit Window Maker. Otherwise, the X server
might be shut down before the script is executed.
.TP
.B ~/.config/WindowMaker/Library/WindowMaker/
The menu file indicated in WMRootMenu is looked for here...
.TP
.B @pkgdatadir@/
and here, in that order. Unless the indicated path is an absolute path.
.TP
.B ~/.config/WindowMaker/Library/WindowMaker/Pixmaps/
Window Maker looks for \fBpixmaps\fP here
.TP
.B ~/.config/WindowMaker/Library/Icons/
Window Maker looks for \fBicons\fP here
.TP
.B ~/.config/WindowMaker/Library/WindowMaker/Backgrounds/
Window Maker looks for backgrounds here
.TP
.B ~/.config/WindowMaker/Library/WindowMaker/Styles/
Window Maker looks for style files here (not true... it looks like it
does, but you have to specify the full path anyway, it's just a place
to keep things nicely ordered)
.TP
.B ~/.config/WindowMaker/Library/WindowMaker/Themes/
Window Maker looks for theme files here (ibid)
.TP
.B @pkgdatadir@/Pixmaps/
System-wide (Window Maker-specific) pixmaps are located here
.TP
.B @pkgdatadir@/Styles/
System-wide styles are here
.TP
.B @pkgdatadir@/Themes/
Guess... ;-)
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.IP WMAKER_USER_ROOT
specifies the per-user configuration root. "Defaults/" is appended to this
variable to determine the actual location of the databases. If the variable
is not set, it defaults to ~/GNUstep when ~/GNUstep/Defaults exists, otherwise
to ${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-~/.config}/WindowMaker. Window Maker exports the
resolved value to child processes.
.IP GNUSTEP_USER_APPS
specifies the location of the user's GNUstep Apps directory. If this
variable is empty, it defaults to $WMAKER_USER_ROOT/Applications.
.IP GNUSTEP_LOCAL_APPS
specifies the location of the system-wide \fBlocal\fP GNUstep Apps
directory (this is useful, for example, in those cases where the
system-wide location is really a network wide location). If this
variable is empty, it defaults to /usr/local/GNUstep/Local/Applications.
.IP GNUSTEP_SYSTEM_APPS
specifies the location of the system-wide GNUstep Apps directory. If this
variable is empty, it defaults to /usr/GNUstep/System/Applications.
